    "sampleAnswerBand8": "In contemporary educational discourse, a widening schism has emerged between the glorification of STEM subjects (science, technology, engineering, and mathematics) and the alleged obsolescence of historical education. While science and technology are undeniably the technological engines of modern economic prosperity, I firmly believe that history is equally indispensable, as it provides the ethical and civilizational compass required to steer scientific power wisely.\n\nOn the one hand, proponents of STEM education argue from a position of economic utility and survival. The modern world is propelled by artificial intelligence, genomic biotechnology, and renewable energy grids; nations that fail to cultivate elite cohorts of software engineers, biochemists, and roboticists inevitably suffer economic stagnation and geopolitical vulnerability. STEM curricula equip young minds with empirical problem-solving faculties, driving the medical therapies that eradicate diseases and the clean technologies required to combat global warming. In a fiercely competitive globalized knowledge economy, prioritizing technical skills appears to be an urgent pragmatic imperative.\n\nOn the other hand, discarding history is a recipe for civilizational suicide. As the philosopher George Santayana famously remarked, 'Those who cannot remember the past are condemned to repeat it.' History is not the dry memorization of antique dates; it is the forensic dissection of human nature, power structures, propaganda, and societal collapse. Studying the historical rise of totalitarian regimes, the horrors of warfare, and the struggles for civil rights equips youth with critical historical consciousness, teaching them to identify demagoguery and cherish democratic institutions. Furthermore, scientific prowess devoid of historical ethics yields dystopian monstrosities—such as atomic weapons, surveillance capitalism, or unethical eugenics.\n\nIn conclusion, pitting science against history represents a false and dangerous dichotomy. While science and technology furnish humanity with the immense technical capability to reshape the physical world, history endows us with the moral wisdom to avoid self-annihilation. Both must remain twin pillars of modern schooling.",
