    "sampleAnswerBand8": "The strategic allocation of national transport budgets has sparked fierce debate over whether governments should channel astronomical capital into building high-speed intercity railway networks or prioritize the modernization of existing local transit systems. While high-speed rail offers compelling macroeconomic and environmental benefits, I firmly contend that renovating everyday urban public transportation delivers vastly more democratic, daily, and transformative benefits to the populace.\n\nOn the one hand, proponents of high-speed rail lines validly highlight their transformative role in regional integration and ecological decarbonization. Bullet trains connecting major commercial metropolises slash travel durations to fractions of automotive journeys, providing a hyper-efficient alternative to domestic aviation. Since electrified rail generates significantly lower carbon emissions per passenger-kilometer than short-haul flights, high-speed networks are pivotal for meeting national climate targets. Furthermore, linking secondary peripheral towns to economic mega-capitals spurs regional economic decentralization, encouraging corporations to establish hubs outside congested megacities.\n\nOn the other hand, the overwhelming majority of citizens do not travel between distant cities on a weekly basis; rather, they rely daily on municipal buses, trams, and suburban commuter trains to access workplaces, schools, and hospitals. When governments pour tens of billions into prestigious high-speed lines—which often carry expensive premium ticket prices accessible only to affluent business travelers—everyday urban transit infrastructure is chronically starved of maintenance. Consequently, millions of ordinary working-class citizens endure over-crowded, unreliable buses, broken subway lines, and paralyzed traffic gridlock on a daily basis. Upgrading localized transit—through dedicated bus rapid transit lanes, modern subway signaling, and electrified commuter fleets—directly alleviates urban pollution and improves the quality of life for the broad demographic majority.\n\nIn conclusion, while high-speed intercity rail is an admirable visionary technology, fiscal priorities must align with the daily needs of citizens. Modernizing, expanding, and subsidizing local public transit infrastructure must take precedence over high-cost intercity rail projects.",
